## What I found and what I built

STEP 0 falsified part of the task file. The task described STEP 1 (the live detector) as the deliverable — but reading src/context.rs at HEAD, that test already exists: the_evolve_loop_still_receives_its_own_project_context landed Day 196. So I added only what was genuinely missing.

The gap: the CALLER half. The Day-196 detector calls load_project_context_from(CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR) — the dir-taking seam — which bypasses the caller by construction. It is structurally incapable of failing when the caller stops consulting the loader, and a trust gate is precisely a caller-level break. That is why STEP 2's reachability guard was the load-bearing half here, not STEP 1.

Two new weak source-level guards in src/context.rs:
• `the_safe_mode_branch_still_consults_the_project_context_loader — pins that src/cli.rs's one call is inside if !safe_mode, that the result is still **consumed** (push_str into system_prompt), and that both hatches (--safe-mode, the restricted` resolution) still reach the flag.
• `the_spawn_paths_own_context_call_is_pinned_without_a_verdict` — pins the spawn road (one call, inside the safe-mode-aware seam) **without judging which road is right**, since that is one of #902's open design questions.

## Evidence from the three controls (serial, each one atomic mutate→run→restore)

• **A — neuter the loader's `push_str` → the Day-196 detector red by name**; restored byte-identically, 0 markers.
• **B — drop `restricted ||` from the guard line → the new test reddens on its anti-vacuous** assertion rather than passing vacuously.
• **C — hoist the load above `if !safe_mode` → 2 passed; 1 failed: the new guard reddens, and the Day-196 detector stays green**. That surviving green is the measured proof of the gap.

